# .mailmap — one person, one name, in every git report.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. People commit under more than one identity: a laptop with a
# different git config, a GitHub web edit using a noreply address. Git treats each
# combination as a separate human, so `git shortlog` and `git log --author` split one
# person's work several ways. This file joins them back up for DISPLAY only. It
# rewrites nothing and changes no commit.
#
# FORMAT:  Proper Name <proper@email>  <committed@email>
#
# NO PERSONAL EMAIL ADDRESSES, ON PURPOSE. This repository is public. This file used
# to list teammates' school and personal addresses so their older commits would be
# joined up; that put addresses they never chose to publish into a file anyone can
# read. Only GitHub's no-reply addresses belong here. The cost is that commits made
# with a personal address show under whatever name they were made with.
#
# Committing from a new machine? Set your GitHub no-reply address first (GitHub →
# Settings → Emails shows it), then add a line here if you need one:
#   git config --global user.email "ID+username@users.noreply.github.com"

